&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ curl -s -X POST https://api.osfoundry.io/api/v1/external-mcp \
    -H 'content-type: application/json' -H 'accept: application/json, text/event-stream' \
    -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/list","params":{}}'
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"propose_gitnest_file_import"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
 &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"description"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Prepare up to 20 UTF-8 files (200 KB total) for a native GitNest repository branch. The owner reviews every file and approves the commit in osFoundry. Repository GitNest must be enabled."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
 &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"inputSchema"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"required"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"repository_id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"branch"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"message"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"files"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"idempotency_key"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
   &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"properties"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
     &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"files"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"minItems"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"maxItems"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
               &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"items"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"properties"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"path"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"maxLength"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2048&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"content"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"maxLength"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;100000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}}}},&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
     &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"idempotency_key"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"string"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"minLength"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"maxLength"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;128&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}}},&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
 &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"annotations"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"readOnlyHint"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"destructiveHint"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"openWorldHint"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
 &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"securitySchemes"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"oauth2"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"scopes"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"osfoundry:write"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]}]}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Achte auf die Verben. Das Tool "bereitet vor", der Owner "genehmigt den Commit". Der Aufruf kehrt zurück, bevor irgendetwas Git anfasst, und es gibt auf dem Connector kein anderes Tool, das Git anfasst. Der &lt;code&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/code&gt; ist Pflicht, nicht optional, und hat eine Mindestlänge. Ein Client kann also keinen leeren oder einstelligen Key schicken und das idempotent nennen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Der Rest des Posts zeigt, warum diese Form für einen Agenten die richtige ist und was der Server tun muss, damit sie verlässlich bleibt. Danach wird die kleinste Version über einem echten Git-Repository nachgebaut und gemessen, was schiefgeht, wenn man Teile weglässt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Was legen die sieben Propose-Tools fest?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;GitNest hat 13 Tools auf dem Connector. Sechs lesen nur: &lt;code&gt;read_gitnest_repository&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;search_gitnest_code&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;list_gitnest_work&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;read_gitnest_work&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;list_gitnest_saved_results&lt;/code&gt; und &lt;code&gt;list_gitnest_checks&lt;/code&gt;. Die anderen sieben beginnen mit &lt;code&gt;propose_&lt;/code&gt;. Ihre Pflichtfelder zeigen, was jedes davon in dem Moment festnagelt, in dem das Modell es aufruft:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Tool&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;code&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;code&gt;expected_change_set_version&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;code&gt;expected_composition_oid&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;code&gt;openWorldHint&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;propose_gitnest_work&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nein&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nein&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;false&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;propose_gitnest_change_set&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nein&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nein&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;false&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;propose_gitnest_server_composition&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nein&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;false&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;propose_gitnest_file_import&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nein&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;nein&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;false&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;propose_gitnest_check_rerun&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;false&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;propose_gitnest_native_publication&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;true&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;propose_gitnest_provider_publication&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pflicht&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;true&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Was festgelegt wird, wächst mit der Tragweite. Eine Aufgabe legt nur ihren Key fest. Das Komponieren legt die Change-Set-Version fest, die das Modell gelesen hat. Einen Check neu starten oder einen Pull Request veröffentlichen legt die Version und den exakten komponierten Commit fest. Beide Publication-Tools sind mit &lt;code&gt;openWorldHint: true&lt;/code&gt; markiert, weil sie etwas außerhalb des Proposal-Stores erzeugen: einen Pull Request und beim Provider-Tool einen Branch, der zu GitHub oder GitLab gepusht wird.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In der Review-Karte des Owners werden diese Werte zu Text: "Change set: … · expected version 3", "Reviewed commit: 9c1e47a…". Genehmigt wird genau diese Version und dieser Commit, nicht das, was gerade aktuell ist, wenn der Klick ankommt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Warum gibt man dem Agenten nicht einfach ein Push-Token?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Weil ein Push in dem Moment entschieden ist, in dem das Modell ihn absetzt, und ein Vorschlag erst dann, wenn ihn ein Mensch mit aktuellen Rechten ansieht.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Der Code von osFoundry zieht diese Grenze ausdrücklich. Die Schreibrouten von GitNest sind durch &lt;code&gt;denyAgentScope&lt;/code&gt; geschützt, und die In-App-Bridge von Maestro zu GitNest erlaubt nur Lesezugriffe. Die Connected Tools umgehen das nicht mit einem geliehenen Browser-Token. Jeder Vorschlag wird gespeichert, dem Owner der Verbindung angezeigt und nach der Genehmigung von osFoundrys eigenem Writer ausgeführt, der Verbindung zugeordnet. Eine Claude-Unterhaltung hält nie ein Credential, das ins Repository schreiben kann, und sieht nie das GitHub- oder GitLab-Token des Owners. Die Provider-Publication pusht nur den dedizierten Branch des Change Sets, mit einem Absent-Ref-Lease, und spiegelt nie fremde Refs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Der Preis dafür ist Zeit. Die Lücke zwischen "vorgeschlagen" und "genehmigt" ist menschliche Zeit: Minuten, manchmal ein Tag. In diesem Fenster bewegt sich der Branch, jemand komponiert das Change Set neu, ein Check wird fertig, der Owner verliert seine Repository-Rolle, die Verbindung wird widerrufen. Die Genehmigung in osFoundry prüft erneut die Verbindung, die Projektmitgliedschaft, die aktuelle Repository-Rolle des Owners, den Ablauf und bei Change-Set-Aktionen die exakte Version und den Commit, bei Publications außerdem die Risk Policy und die Pflicht-Checks. "A declined, expired, or stale proposal must not execute" steht als Zeile in seinem Plan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Zwei Fehlerfälle bleiben übrig, und um die geht es im Nachbau:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Zwei Genehmigungen auf derselben Basis. Jede sieht für sich allein in Ordnung aus.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ein Retry nach einem teilweisen Fehler. Git hat den Commit, die Datenbank weiß es noch nicht, und der Owner klickt noch einmal.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Für den zweiten beschreibt der osFoundry-Plan den Fix, der im Git-Service ausgeliefert wurde: Datei-Importe übergeben die unveränderliche Proposal-ID als Idempotency-Key, und der Service "updates the branch and replay marker atomically with compare-and-swap, so a database failure after the Git commit can be retried without another commit."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Der Nachbau
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Zwei Dateien, Standardbibliothek plus git-CLI (Apple Git 2.39.5), ausgeführt auf einem Mac mit Apple M1, der nebenbei Videos für andere Jobs gerendert hat:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;server.py&lt;/code&gt;, 225 Zeilen: ein JSON-RPC-Endpoint über einem Bare-Git-Repository und einer sqlite-Datei.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;client.py&lt;/code&gt;, 203 Zeilen: ein geskripteter "Claude" und ein geskripteter Owner.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Weder ein Claude-Binary noch ein Claude-Account war beteiligt. Der Client schickt dieselbe Art von &lt;code&gt;tools/call&lt;/code&gt;-Requests, die ein verbundener Chat schicken würde. Die Tools des Nachbaus sind &lt;code&gt;read_repo&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;propose_file_import&lt;/code&gt; und &lt;code&gt;read_proposal&lt;/code&gt;. Diese Namen gehören zum Nachbau, und keines davon schreibt in Git. Der Owner entscheidet auf einer separaten Route, &lt;code&gt;/owner/decide&lt;/code&gt;, die auf ein Agent-Token mit &lt;code&gt;403 agent_scope_denied&lt;/code&gt; antwortet. Das ist die Idee von &lt;code&gt;denyAgentScope&lt;/code&gt; in einem einzigen &lt;code&gt;if&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ein bewusster Unterschied: Der Nachbau legt auch bei Datei-Importen einen Basis-Commit fest (&lt;code&gt;expected_base_oid&lt;/code&gt;, standardmäßig die Branch-Spitze zum Zeitpunkt des Vorschlags). Das Schema von &lt;code&gt;propose_gitnest_file_import&lt;/code&gt; in osFoundry hat kein solches Feld. Betrachte diesen Teil als Designentscheidung des Nachbaus, nicht als Beschreibung von osFoundry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Die Annahme von Vorschlägen ist idempotent auf &lt;code&gt;(connection, idempotency_key)&lt;/code&gt;, mit einem Hash des Bodys, damit ein wiederverwendeter Key mit anderem Body ein Fehler ist und kein stiller Replay:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;execute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;INSERT OR IGNORE INTO proposals (id,conn,idem_key,body_hash,branch,base_oid,files,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;message,status,created_at,expires_at) VALUES (?,?,?,?,?,?,?,?,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;pending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;,?,?)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(...))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;execute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;SELECT * FROM proposals WHERE conn=? AND idem_key=?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;conn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetchone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;body_hash&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Conflict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;idempotency_key_reused_with_different_body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Die Genehmigung baut den Commit nur mit Plumbing-Befehlen (&lt;code&gt;read-tree&lt;/code&gt; in einen temporären Index, &lt;code&gt;hash-object -w&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;update-index --cacheinfo&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;write-tree&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;commit-tree -p &amp;lt;base&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;). Nichts davon bewegt eine Ref. Danach bewegt eine einzige Transaktion den Branch nur, wenn er noch auf die geprüfte Basis zeigt, und legt eine Marker-Ref mit dem Namen des Vorschlags an:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;update refs/heads/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;branch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;base_oid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;create refs/proposals/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;proposal_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;prepare&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;commit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;git&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;update-ref&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;--stdin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;inp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;update&lt;/code&gt; mit einem alten Wert ist Compare-and-Swap. &lt;code&gt;create&lt;/code&gt; schlägt fehl, wenn die Ref schon existiert. Git wendet beides an oder keins. Bevor irgendetwas gebaut wird, sucht die Genehmigung nach &lt;code&gt;refs/proposals/&amp;lt;id&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. Ist die Ref da, hat ein früherer Versuch Git schon erreicht. Dann wird dieser Commit gespeichert und als Replay zurückgegeben.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Was der geskriptete Claude gesehen hat
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;python3 client.py scenario&lt;/code&gt;, mit gekürzten Proposal-UUIDs. Die Ablauf-Zeile stammt von einem zweiten Server, der mit einer TTL von 0.3 s gestartet wurde:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;read_repo                          -&amp;gt; head 29a940e8  (1 commit)
propose A  README.md  key=readme-fix-0001 -&amp;gt; {"proposal_id": "96dcf05f-…", "status": "pending", "base_oid": "29a940e8"}
propose A  (retry, same key+body)  -&amp;gt; {"proposal_id": "96dcf05f-…", "status": "pending", "base_oid": "29a940e8", "replayed": true}
propose A' (same key, other body)  -&amp;gt; idempotency_key_reused_with_different_body
propose B  app.py     key=app-bump-0001   -&amp;gt; {"proposal_id": "c91824e3-…", "status": "pending", "base_oid": "29a940e8"}
decide A with the agent token      -&amp;gt; HTTP 403 {'error': 'agent_scope_denied'}
owner approves A                   -&amp;gt; HTTP 200 {"status": "executed", "commit": "f07b9fda"}  (2 commits)
owner approves B                   -&amp;gt; HTTP 200 {"status": "stale", "refused": true, "base_oid": "29a940e8", "branch_head": "f07b9fda"}  (2 commits)
propose B2 on new head f07b9fda    -&amp;gt; {"proposal_id": "bc3cedfc-…", "status": "pending", "base_oid": "f07b9fda"}
owner approves B2, crash before DB -&amp;gt; HTTP 500 {'error': 'injected: crash after git, before DB'}  (3 commits)
owner retries approval of B2       -&amp;gt; HTTP 200 {"status": "executed", "commit": "fb736eda", "replayed": true}  (3 commits)
owner clicks approve on A again    -&amp;gt; HTTP 200 {"status": "executed", "commit": "f07b9fda", "replayed": true}  (3 commits)
approve after decline              -&amp;gt; HTTP 200 {'status': 'declined', 'refused': True}  (3 commits)
approve after expiry (ttl 0.3 s)   -&amp;gt; HTTP 200 {'status': 'expired', 'refused': True}  (1 commit)

fb736ed Claude (conn-claude-research) | repo-owner | Bump app
f07b9fd Claude (conn-claude-research) | repo-owner | Pin install version
29a940e owner | owner | seed
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;B hat &lt;code&gt;app.py&lt;/code&gt; geändert und A &lt;code&gt;README.md&lt;/code&gt;, und trotzdem wurde B abgelehnt. Das ist die strenge Lesart von "der Owner hat das gegen 29a940e8 geprüft". Der Fix ist billig: Der Client liest die neue Spitze und schlägt erneut vor, und genau das ist B2. Die &lt;code&gt;git log&lt;/code&gt;-Zeile zeigt die Herkunft, die der Store schreibt. Author ist die Verbindung, Committer ist die Person, die genehmigt hat, und die Message trägt die Trailer &lt;code&gt;Proposal: &amp;lt;id&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; und &lt;code&gt;Approved-by:&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Was geht ohne Compare-and-Swap kaputt?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;--mode naive&lt;/code&gt; behält alles außer der Transaktion. Es prüft die Spitze in Python, baut den Commit und führt dann &lt;code&gt;git update-ref refs/heads/main &amp;lt;new&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; aus, ohne alten Wert und ohne Marker. Gleicher Server, gleicher Client, gleiches Repository-Layout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Race-Test: zwei verschiedene Vorschläge auf derselben Basis, im selben Moment aus zwei Threads genehmigt (&lt;code&gt;threading.Barrier&lt;/code&gt;), 100 Versuche pro Lauf, zwei Läufe. Nach jedem Versuch fragt der Client per &lt;code&gt;git merge-base --is-ancestor&lt;/code&gt;, ob jeder als ausgeführt gemeldete Commit wirklich auf &lt;code&gt;main&lt;/code&gt; liegt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Crash-Test: genehmigen mit einem eingeschleusten Fehler nach dem Git-Schritt und vor dem Datenbank-Update, dann erneut genehmigen. 50 Versuche pro Lauf, zwei Läufe. Der Client zählt die Commits auf &lt;code&gt;main&lt;/code&gt; vorher und nachher.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Modus&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Race: beide als "executed" gemeldet&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Race: genehmigte Commits nicht auf main&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Crash, dann Retry&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Zusätzliche Commits nach Retry&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;CAS + Marker, Lauf 1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0 / 100&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50 × executed (replayed)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;CAS + Marker, Lauf 2&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0 / 100&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50 × executed (replayed)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;naive, Lauf 1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;100 / 100&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;100&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50 × stale&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;naive, Lauf 2&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;100 / 100&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;100&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50 × stale&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Das naive Race ist in jedem Versuch gescheitert, nicht nur gelegentlich. Das Fenster zwischen "Spitze gleich Basis" und "Ref schreiben" ist vier git-Prozesse lang, einige zehn Millisekunden, und zwei Threads, die von einer Barrier losgelassen werden, landen jedes Mal beide darin. Beide Genehmigungen liefern &lt;code&gt;executed&lt;/code&gt; mit einer Commit-ID. Der Owner sieht zwei grüne Ergebnisse, und einer der beiden Commits ist nicht in der History des Branches. Nichts wirft einen Fehler. Mit Compare-and-Swap endete jeder Versuch mit genau einem ausgeführten Vorschlag und einer &lt;code&gt;stale&lt;/code&gt;-Ablehnung, die den neuen Head nennt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Der naive Crash-Retry hat nicht doppelt committet. Die Basisprüfung hat ihn abgefangen, weil sich der Branch bewegt hatte. Aber sie hat ihn als &lt;code&gt;stale&lt;/code&gt; abgefangen. Der Commit des Vorschlags selbst liegt auf &lt;code&gt;main&lt;/code&gt;, und der Store meldet diesen Vorschlag jetzt als abgelehnt. Wenn du die Basisprüfung entfernst, damit das verschwindet, schreibt der Retry den Commit ein zweites Mal. Erst der Replay-Marker erlaubt dem Retry zu sagen: "Das ist schon passiert, hier ist der Commit."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Was kostet das?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Wall-Clock-Zeit pro Request vom Client über Loopback-HTTP (urllib, jedes Mal eine neue Verbindung), n = 200 pro Operation und Lauf, zwei Läufe. Die Bereiche gehen von Lauf 1 bis Lauf 2.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Operation&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;CAS Median&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;CAS p95&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;naive Median&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;naive p95&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;propose (neuer Key)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4.42–4.44 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;7.1–12.3 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4.08–4.66 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;6.4–10.0 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;propose (gleicher Key, Replay)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.42–1.50 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3.3–4.5 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.27–1.49 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2.4–4.1 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;approve, wird ausgeführt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;115–119 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;199–257 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;92–105 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;130–193 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;approve, als stale abgelehnt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;28–31 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;52–70 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;14–17 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;24–34 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;approve erneut, schon ausgeführt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.46–1.60 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3.4–4.7 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.38–1.54 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2.6–4.2 ms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fast alles davon sind Prozessstarts. Eine ausführende CAS-Genehmigung startet acht git-Prozesse (Marker-Lookup, Tip-Lookup, &lt;code&gt;read-tree&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;hash-object&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;update-index&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;write-tree&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;commit-tree&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;update-ref --stdin&lt;/code&gt;), die naive sechs. Eine Stale-Ablehnung sind zwei Lookups gegenüber einem. Bei rund 14 ms pro git-Prozess auf dieser ausgelasteten Maschine kostet der sichere Pfad etwa zwei zusätzliche Prozessstarts, um die 15–25 ms, bei einem Request, der ohnehin auf einen menschlichen Klick wartet. Ein Server, der libgit2 linkt, statt Shell-Aufrufe zu machen, würde alle diese Spalten verkürzen. Der Unterschied zwischen ihnen bestünde weiterhin aus den zwei Lookups.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Replays sind in beiden Tabellen der billigste Pfad, ein einziger sqlite-Read. Ein Client, der einen Retry macht, kostet den Server weniger als ein neuer Request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Wenn du das selbst baust
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Gib dem Agenten kein Schreib-Tool für das Repository, nur &lt;code&gt;propose_*&lt;/code&gt;, und prüfe auf der Entscheidungsroute, dass der Aufrufer kein Agent-Token ist. Verlass dich nicht darauf, dass der Agent die Route nicht findet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Leg im Vorschlag fest, was der Reviewer gesehen hat, Version und Commit, und vergleiche es bei der Genehmigung. Lehne mit dem aktuellen Head in der Antwort ab, damit der Client neu lesen und erneut vorschlagen kann.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Mach den Git-Write zu einer einzigen Ref-Transaktion: Branch-Update mit dem alten Wert plus ein Create-only-Marker mit dem Namen des Vorschlags. Such nach dem Marker, bevor du irgendetwas baust.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Verlange einen Idempotency-Key mit Mindestlänge, speichere ihn mit einem Hash des Bodys und lehne einen wiederverwendeten Key mit anderem Body ab.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Schreib die Herkunft in den Commit selbst. Author ist die Verbindung, Committer ist die genehmigende Person, und ein Trailer trägt die Proposal-ID. &lt;code&gt;git log&lt;/code&gt; hat das noch, wenn deine Datenbank längst weg ist.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hör beim Pull Request auf. Merge und Deploy gehören dem, dem das Repository gehört, auf seinem eigenen Bildschirm.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Den GitNest-Teil davon, also Clone, Push und Access Tokens, beschreibt osFoundry auf der &lt;a href="https://osfoundry.io/de/features/gitnest" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitNest-Seite&lt;/a&gt;. Der Connector spricht einfaches &lt;a href="https://osfoundry.io/de/glossary/mcp" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MCP&lt;/a&gt;, deshalb funktionieren dieselben Propose-Tools auch aus Claude Code oder jedem anderen MCP-Host.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;이 글을 읽고 있다면 지금 탭이 40개는 넘을 겁니다. 크롬이 램 4GB를 먹고 있고, 노트북 팬은 비명을 지르고, 20분 전에 연 그 탭은 어디 갔는지 모르겠죠.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;그래서 만든 게 &lt;strong&gt;Tabs in One&lt;/strong&gt;입니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  문제
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;크롬 탭 바는 가로로 한 줄입니다. 탭이 열 개를 넘으면 각각이 얇은 조각으로 줄어듭니다. 뭐가 열려 있는지 놓치고, 중복이 쌓이고, 램 사용량이 올라갑니다. 탭 관리 확장 프로그램이 없는 건 아니지만 대부분은:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;콘텐츠와 자리를 다투는 사이드바를 하나 더 붙이거나,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;기본 기능에 계정이나 구독을 요구하거나,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;아니면 이미 방치된 상태입니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Tabs in One이 하는 일
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tabs in One은 &lt;strong&gt;열려 있는 모든 탭을 깔끔한 한 줄짜리 페이지 하나로 모읍니다&lt;/strong&gt;. 실행하면 각 탭이 그 페이지로 옮겨지고 원래 탭은 닫히면서 램이 바로 확보됩니다. 어떤 탭이든(또는 전부) 클릭 한 번으로 일반 탭으로 되돌릴 수 있고, 잃는 것은 없습니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;주요 기능:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;한 줄 보기&lt;/strong&gt; — 각 탭이 제목과 주소가 있는 읽기 좋은 행으로 표시됩니다. 작은 파비콘과는 작별입니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;클릭 한 번으로 복원&lt;/strong&gt; — 하나씩 또는 한꺼번에.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;중복 링크 없음&lt;/strong&gt; — 관리 페이지가 이미 열려 있으면 새 링크만 추가됩니다. 잃는 것도, 중복도 없습니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;백업&lt;/strong&gt; — 매일 자동(최근 5개 보관)에 더해 수동 «지금 백업».&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;내보내기 / 가져오기&lt;/strong&gt; — JSON 또는 HTML. 탭 목록을 다른 PC로 옮길 수 있습니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;통계와 등급&lt;/strong&gt; — 저장한 탭 수, 확보한 램, 개인 최고 기록. «Neat Tabber»부터 «Final Boss of RAM»까지 등급이 있습니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;기록 공유&lt;/strong&gt; — 공유용 이미지와 링크를 만들어 줍니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  사용법
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tabs in One 아이콘을 클릭합니다(또는 우클릭 메뉴의 «Open Tabs in One»).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;모든 탭이 한 줄 페이지로 이동하고 원래 탭은 닫히며 램이 확보됩니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;보고, 찾고, 원할 때 복원하세요.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;사용 권한: &lt;code&gt;tabs&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;storage&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;contextMenus&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;alarms&lt;/code&gt;. 계정도, 구독도 없고, 데이터는 브라우저 밖으로 나가지 않습니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  솔직한 단점
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;탭을 읽고 관리하려면 &lt;code&gt;tabs&lt;/code&gt; 권한이 필요합니다. 그게 목적이지만 일부 사용자는 꺼립니다. 코드는 누구나 확인할 수 있습니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;고정한 탭이 계속 고정돼 있어야 한다면 복원 동작을 먼저 확인하세요.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;이건 탭 &lt;em&gt;관리&lt;/em&gt; 도구이지 세션 동기화 도구가 아닙니다. 기기 간 동기화는 되지 않습니다(내보내기/가져오기를 쓰세요).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  설치
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chrome 웹 스토어: &lt;a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/ehghdjiblhadojkedfjkmbeohiphogdb" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/ehghdjiblhadojkedfjkmbeohiphogdb&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;무료. 계정 필요 없음.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  이런 분께
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;참고 탭을 30개 넘게 띄워 두고 일하는 연구자와 작가.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;문서, Stack Overflow, GitHub를 동시에 오가는 개발자.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;크롬이 잡아먹는 램 때문에 팬이 비명을 지르는 모든 분.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;일주일 써 보세요. 시간이 안 아껴지면 삭제하면 됩니다. 비용은 0원입니다.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Chrome Manifest V3로 만들었습니다. 피드백 환영합니다.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My API worked every time on my laptop. A new teammate cloned the repo, ran &lt;code&gt;docker compose up&lt;/code&gt;, and got &lt;code&gt;ECONNREFUSED 172.18.0.2:5432&lt;/code&gt; on the first try. CI hit the same error about once a week. When I ran it again it was fine, and that's the most annoying kind of bug there is.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cause was &lt;strong&gt;Docker Compose depends_on&lt;/strong&gt;, which does less than its name suggests. Then I fixed it with a Postgres healthcheck, and that healthcheck lied too, but only on a fresh volume. That explained why it never broke for me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wrote a loop that ran &lt;code&gt;docker compose down -v &amp;amp;&amp;amp; docker compose up&lt;/code&gt; 40 times for each config. Here's what I found.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;depends_on: [db]&lt;/code&gt; (short syntax) waits until the db container is &lt;strong&gt;started&lt;/strong&gt;. It does not wait for Postgres to accept connections. With that config, 11 of my 40 fresh starts crashed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;To wait for readiness, use the long syntax with &lt;code&gt;condition: service_healthy&lt;/code&gt; and give the db service a &lt;code&gt;healthcheck&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;pg_isready&lt;/code&gt; with no &lt;code&gt;-h&lt;/code&gt; checks the &lt;strong&gt;Unix socket&lt;/strong&gt;. During first-boot init, the official Postgres image runs a temporary server that listens only on the socket. So the check passes before TCP is open, and before your init SQL has finished. Use &lt;code&gt;pg_isready -h 127.0.0.1&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Healthcheck &lt;code&gt;interval&lt;/code&gt; defaults to &lt;strong&gt;30s&lt;/strong&gt;, so a basic healthcheck adds about 30 seconds to every &lt;code&gt;up&lt;/code&gt;. Set it to &lt;code&gt;1s&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run migrations as a one-shot service and gate the app on &lt;code&gt;condition: service_completed_successfully&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What does Docker Compose depends_on actually wait for?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The short form of Docker Compose depends_on controls &lt;strong&gt;start order&lt;/strong&gt;. Compose creates and starts &lt;code&gt;db&lt;/code&gt;, then starts &lt;code&gt;api&lt;/code&gt; right after. It doesn't check whether the process inside &lt;code&gt;db&lt;/code&gt; is ready to serve anything.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;services&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;image&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;postgres:16&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;environment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;POSTGRES_PASSWORD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;dev&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;POSTGRES_DB&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;api&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;build&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;depends_on&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A Postgres container is "started" when its entrypoint process is running. On a fresh volume, that entrypoint still has to run &lt;code&gt;initdb&lt;/code&gt;, start a temporary server, run your init scripts, shut the temporary server down, and start the real one. That takes a few seconds. My Node API connects within milliseconds of starting, so it gets there first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The short syntax is the same as writing:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;depends_on&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;condition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;service_started&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;service_started&lt;/code&gt; is the default, and it isn't the same as "ready".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My numbers for the short syntax, fresh volume each run:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Config&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Crashed on startup&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;depends_on: [db]&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;11 / 40&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The other 29 runs only worked because Docker happened to be slow enough that time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How do you make Docker Compose wait until Postgres is ready?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add a &lt;code&gt;healthcheck&lt;/code&gt; to the database and use &lt;code&gt;condition: service_healthy&lt;/code&gt; on the service that depends on it. Compose then won't start &lt;code&gt;api&lt;/code&gt; until Docker marks &lt;code&gt;db&lt;/code&gt; as healthy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most tutorials give you something like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;services&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;image&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;postgres:16&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;environment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;POSTGRES_PASSWORD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;dev&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;POSTGRES_DB&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;healthcheck&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;CMD-SHELL"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;pg_isready&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-U&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;postgres&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;app"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;api&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;build&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;depends_on&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;condition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;service_healthy&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That fixed the crashes. It also made every &lt;code&gt;docker compose up&lt;/code&gt; take about 31 seconds, and I hadn't changed anything else.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why does my healthcheck make docker compose up take 30 seconds?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Docker's healthcheck &lt;code&gt;interval&lt;/code&gt; defaults to 30 seconds, and the first probe runs one interval after the container starts. So Postgres is ready in about 2 seconds, Docker waits until second 30 to check, and your app waits along with it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here are the defaults you get when you leave the fields out:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Field&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Default&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;interval&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;30s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;timeout&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;30s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;retries&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;start_period&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set the interval explicitly:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;healthcheck&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;CMD-SHELL"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;pg_isready&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-U&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;postgres&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;app"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;interval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;1s&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;3s&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;retries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;up&lt;/code&gt; went from about 31s back to about 3s. Running &lt;code&gt;pg_isready&lt;/code&gt; once a second is cheap, so I see no reason to run it less often in dev.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then I ran my 40-run loop again, and 4 runs still crashed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why does pg_isready say healthy while connections are still refused?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;pg_isready&lt;/code&gt; with no &lt;code&gt;-h&lt;/code&gt; flag connects through the local &lt;strong&gt;Unix domain socket&lt;/strong&gt;, not TCP. The official Postgres image's entrypoint does its first-boot setup on a temporary server started with &lt;code&gt;listen_addresses=''&lt;/code&gt;, which means socket only and no TCP at all. So while init is running, the socket check reports "accepting connections" and port 5432 isn't open yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's what happens on a fresh volume:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;initdb&lt;/code&gt; creates the data directory.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A temporary server starts, listening on the socket only.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Everything in &lt;code&gt;/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/&lt;/code&gt; runs against it: your schema, seed data, extensions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The temporary server shuts down.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The real server starts and listens on TCP.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the healthcheck runs during step 2 or 3, it passes. Docker marks the container healthy, Compose starts the API, and the API tries TCP and gets refused. Or it connects in the short gap after step 5 and finds a database whose init scripts haven't finished.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This also explains why it never broke on my machine. My volume already existed, so the entrypoint skipped init completely. There was no temporary server and no gap. It only failed on a fresh volume, which is exactly what a new teammate or a CI runner has.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is one flag. Make the check use TCP, the same way your app connects:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;healthcheck&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;CMD-SHELL"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;pg_isready&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;127.0.0.1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-U&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;postgres&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;app"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;interval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;1s&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;3s&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;retries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The temporary server has no TCP listener, so this check can't pass until step 5, and by then init is done.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Config&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Crashed on startup&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Avg &lt;code&gt;up&lt;/code&gt; time&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;short &lt;code&gt;depends_on&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;11 / 40&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~2s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;healthcheck, default interval&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0 / 40&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~31s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;healthcheck, &lt;code&gt;interval: 1s&lt;/code&gt;, socket&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4 / 40&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~3s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;healthcheck, &lt;code&gt;interval: 1s&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;-h 127.0.0.1&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0 / 40&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~3s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What about the $POSTGRES_USER trap in the healthcheck?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compose interpolates &lt;code&gt;$VARIABLES&lt;/code&gt; in your YAML from your &lt;strong&gt;host&lt;/strong&gt; shell and &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; file before the container exists. If you write &lt;code&gt;pg_isready -U $POSTGRES_USER&lt;/code&gt;, Compose tries to fill it in from your laptop's environment, and you'll get a warning like "The POSTGRES_USER variable is not set. Defaulting to a blank string."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To pass a literal &lt;code&gt;$&lt;/code&gt; through so the container's shell expands it, double it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;CMD-SHELL"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;pg_isready&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;127.0.0.1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-U&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;$${POSTGRES_USER}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;$${POSTGRES_DB}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This one rarely breaks anything, because &lt;code&gt;pg_isready&lt;/code&gt; only checks whether the server accepts connections and doesn't care whether the user exists. That's why it's easy to miss. A healthcheck that passes for the wrong reason will eventually let something through that it shouldn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How do you run migrations before the app starts in Docker Compose?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Make migrations a separate one-shot service, and gate the app on &lt;code&gt;condition: service_completed_successfully&lt;/code&gt;. Compose waits for that container to exit with code 0. If it exits non-zero, the app never starts, which is what you want.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the full file I ended up with:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;services&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;image&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;postgres:16&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;environment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;POSTGRES_USER&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;postgres&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;POSTGRES_PASSWORD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;dev&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;POSTGRES_DB&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;volumes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;./init:/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d:ro&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;healthcheck&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;CMD-SHELL"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;pg_isready&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;127.0.0.1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-U&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;$${POSTGRES_USER}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;-d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;$${POSTGRES_DB}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;interval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;1s&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;3s&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;retries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="na"&gt;migrate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;build&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;command&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;npm run migrate&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;depends_on&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;condition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;service_healthy&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="na"&gt;api&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;build&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;ports&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;3000:3000"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;depends_on&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;condition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;service_healthy&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;migrate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="na"&gt;condition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;service_completed_successfully&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="na"&gt;volumes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;pgdata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In CI, use &lt;code&gt;docker compose up -d --wait&lt;/code&gt;. It blocks until the services are running and healthy, and exits non-zero if one fails, so your test step doesn't race the stack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Should the app still retry its database connection?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Docker Compose depends_on only applies at &lt;strong&gt;startup&lt;/strong&gt;. If Postgres restarts later because of an OOM kill, a config reload, or someone running &lt;code&gt;docker compose restart db&lt;/code&gt;, Compose won't hold your API back, and the API will hit the same refused connection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I added a small retry with backoff to the connection pool: 10 attempts, starting at 200ms and doubling up to 2s. It's about fifteen lines. The healthcheck handles the common case of a cold start, and the retry handles the database restarting later. You want both. Don't use &lt;code&gt;restart: on-failure&lt;/code&gt; as your only fix. It hides the crash loop in &lt;code&gt;docker compose logs&lt;/code&gt; and makes your startup timing random.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Reproduce it yourself
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;i &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;seq &lt;/span&gt;1 40&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;do
  &lt;/span&gt;docker compose down &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-v&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;/dev/null 2&amp;gt;&amp;amp;1
  docker compose up &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-d&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;/dev/null 2&amp;gt;&amp;amp;1
  &lt;span class="nb"&gt;sleep &lt;/span&gt;8
  docker compose ps api &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--format&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{{.State}}'&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-q&lt;/span&gt; running &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo &lt;/span&gt;ok &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo &lt;/span&gt;CRASH
&lt;span class="k"&gt;done&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;uniq&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-c&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;-v&lt;/code&gt; matters. Without it the volume survives between runs, init gets skipped, and the socket bug goes away. That's the same reason I didn't catch it on my own machine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  So does Docker Compose depends_on wait for the database?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. On its own, Docker Compose depends_on only waits for the dependency's container to start, not for the service inside to accept connections. To actually wait, add a &lt;code&gt;healthcheck&lt;/code&gt; to the database with a short &lt;code&gt;interval&lt;/code&gt; (the 30s default makes every &lt;code&gt;up&lt;/code&gt; slow), use &lt;code&gt;depends_on&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;condition: service_healthy&lt;/code&gt;, and with Postgres, run &lt;code&gt;pg_isready -h 127.0.0.1&lt;/code&gt; so the check goes over TCP. The socket-only temporary server the image uses during first-boot init will pass a socket check before the database is really ready. Put migrations in a one-shot service behind &lt;code&gt;service_completed_successfully&lt;/code&gt;, and keep a connection retry in the app for restarts that happen after startup.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Written by the developer behind &lt;a href="https://preterview.com/en" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Preterview&lt;/a&gt;, an interview prep platform.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hub-and-spoke is the default network design for most Azure landing zones, and the basic version is easy: a hub VNet, some spoke VNets, and peerings between them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The problems show up later, usually the first time you add a third spoke, turn on a firewall, or wonder why two spokes can't talk to each other. Here are five gotchas worth knowing before they bite, with the Bicep to avoid each one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. Deploying several peerings at once fails at random
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you create peerings in a loop, you'll eventually see this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;AnotherOperationInProgress: Another operation on this or dependent resource is in progress.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Every peering is a child of the hub VNet, and Azure only allows one update to a VNet at a time. A Bicep loop deploys in parallel by default, so several peerings hit the hub together and some fail. It's intermittent, which makes it extra annoying.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix is one decorator that makes the loop run one at a time:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight bicep"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@batchSize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;resource&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hubToSpoke&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/virtualNetworkPeerings@2023-05-01'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;spoke&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;spokes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="py"&gt;parent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hub&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="py"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'peer-hub-to-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;spoke&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="py"&gt;properties&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="py"&gt;remoteVirtualNetwork&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="py"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;spokeVnets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="py"&gt;allowVirtualNetworkAccess&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="py"&gt;allowForwardedTraffic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It's slightly slower to deploy, but it never fails for this reason.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Peering is not transitive
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Spoke A is peered with the hub. Spoke B is peered with the hub. Spoke A still &lt;strong&gt;cannot&lt;/strong&gt; reach spoke B. Peering only connects the two VNets it's between.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You have two options:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Route through the hub.&lt;/strong&gt; Put Azure Firewall (or another network virtual appliance) in the hub, add a route table on each spoke sending traffic for the other spokes to the firewall's private IP, and allow the traffic in a firewall rule. This is the usual answer because it gives you one place to inspect and log traffic.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Peer the spokes directly.&lt;/strong&gt; Fine for two or three spokes, but it gets messy fast: with 10 spokes, a full mesh is 45 peerings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Whichever you choose, set &lt;code&gt;allowForwardedTraffic: true&lt;/code&gt; on the peerings. Without it, traffic that came through the firewall is dropped.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Hard-coded subnet ranges drift
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most examples hard-code subnet prefixes:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight bicep"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;addressPrefix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'10.0.1.0/26'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That's fine until someone changes the hub's address space and forgets one subnet, which then sits outside the VNet range and fails to deploy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Bicep's &lt;code&gt;cidrSubnet()&lt;/code&gt; function calculates subnets from the parent range instead:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight bicep"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;param&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hubAddressSpace&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'10.0.0.0/22'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sharedSubnetPrefix&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;cidrSubnet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hubAddressSpace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;24&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 10.0.0.0/24&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;firewallSubnetPrefix&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;cidrSubnet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hubAddressSpace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;26&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 10.0.1.0/26&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bastionSubnetPrefix&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;cidrSubnet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hubAddressSpace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;26&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 10.0.1.64/26&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The arguments are the parent range, the new prefix length, and which subnet of that size you want (counting from zero). Change &lt;code&gt;hubAddressSpace&lt;/code&gt; and every subnet follows automatically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Forced tunnelling needs BGP propagation turned off
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To send all spoke internet traffic through the hub firewall, you add a route table with a default route:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight bicep"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;resource&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;spokeRouteTable&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Microsoft.Network/routeTables@2023-05-01'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="py"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'rt-spokes'&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="py"&gt;location&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;location&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="py"&gt;properties&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="py"&gt;disableBgpRoutePropagation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="py"&gt;routes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="py"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'default-via-firewall'&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="py"&gt;properties&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="py"&gt;addressPrefix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'0.0.0.0/0'&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="py"&gt;nextHopType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'VirtualAppliance'&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="py"&gt;nextHopIpAddress&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;firewall&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;properties&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ipConfigurations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;properties&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;privateIPAddress&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Note &lt;code&gt;disableBgpRoutePropagation: true&lt;/code&gt;. If you have a VPN or ExpressRoute gateway, it can advertise routes that are more specific than your &lt;code&gt;0.0.0.0/0&lt;/code&gt;, and those win. Turning off propagation on spoke route tables keeps traffic going through the firewall.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  5. A new firewall blocks everything
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This one surprises people the first time: a brand-new Azure Firewall policy has &lt;strong&gt;no allow rules&lt;/strong&gt;. The moment you add the forced-tunnelling route above, every outbound call from the spokes fails, including package installs, Windows Update and calls to Azure services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before pointing spokes at the firewall, add at least:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an &lt;strong&gt;application rule collection&lt;/strong&gt; for the domains your workloads need (FQDN tags such as &lt;code&gt;WindowsUpdate&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;AzureKubernetesService&lt;/code&gt; help here),&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;network rules&lt;/strong&gt; for spoke-to-spoke traffic, if you're routing it through the hub.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also make Azure Firewall and Bastion optional parameters in your templates. Both are billed hourly even when idle, and test environments rarely need them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Quick checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] &lt;code&gt;@batchSize(1)&lt;/code&gt; on peering loops&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] &lt;code&gt;allowForwardedTraffic: true&lt;/code&gt; on hub ⇄ spoke peerings&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] A plan for spoke-to-spoke traffic (through the firewall, or direct peering)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Subnets calculated with &lt;code&gt;cidrSubnet()&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] &lt;code&gt;disableBgpRoutePropagation: true&lt;/code&gt; on spoke route tables when forcing traffic through a firewall&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Allow rules in place &lt;strong&gt;before&lt;/strong&gt; turning on forced tunnelling&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Firewall and Bastion behind parameters so they're off by default&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I've packaged a hub-and-spoke template with all of the above (any number of spokes, optional firewall and Bastion, calculated subnets) in the &lt;a href="https://aabdansa.gumroad.com/l/azure-bicep-starter-pack" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Azure Bicep Starter Pack&lt;/a&gt;, together with a passwordless Web App + SQL template, a locked-down storage template and a PDF guide. If you'd like to try one first, the &lt;a href="https://aabdansa.gumroad.com/l/free-azure-storage-private-endpoint" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;storage + private endpoint template is free&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I run a personal daily newspaper, published twice a day: a morning edition at 06:00 and an evening edition at 18:00. It includes posts on X from a streamer I follow. The developer dashboard, as of 2026-10-28, showed 247 billable events over the preceding 30 days for fetching those posts, totaling about $1.25.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That figure comes from deciding the window and the target accounts before fetching: "yesterday, 00:00 to 24:00". On the other hand, on a day when I fetched with no window and no cap on the count (2026-11-06, in work separate from the newspaper), fetching profiles and post lists resulted in about 1,000 reads, including reposts, and a $10 charge.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A pay-per-use API isn't something to fear. It's something to manage with a window and a count. This article walks through the design of the fetcher that makes that management work, and the real code (it runs on the Python standard library alone), one decision at a time. If you want to collect posts from specific accounts regularly with the X API, you can apply these design choices to your own system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where the fetcher sits in the publishing pipeline
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, the big picture. The newspaper is published automatically by a scheduled job and deployed to Cloudflare Pages. I started a trial run on 2026-10-26 and have published every day since 2026-11-02.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One issue goes through these steps:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Read back the voting data&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Collect source material&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fetch and enrich X posts&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build the issue data (JSON)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Generate the site&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Push to GitHub&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Publish on Cloudflare Pages&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This article covers only step 3, "fetch and enrich X posts". I split it out of the newspaper as a standalone tool (I call it x-posts-fetcher) and maintain it locally. Selecting articles, generating pages, and publishing are out of scope here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The newspaper has a section that gathers posts from the streamer I follow. The targets are two accounts: a main account and a secondary one. I don't name the accounts or handles in this article. The code fragments and JSON values are fictional, there only to show the format.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The design core: window, accounts, and a single JSON output
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I made three core design decisions in the fetcher:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Decide the window first.&lt;/strong&gt; The fetch period is fixed before any API call.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Define the target accounts in configuration.&lt;/strong&gt; Specify whose posts to fetch in the settings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Funnel the output into one JSON document.&lt;/strong&gt; The newspaper reads only that JSON.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These three choices limit the two sources of higher cost — fetching too long a period and fetching too many accounts — and give the downstream step (building the pages) a single point of contact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also added two refinements while running it: prefetching the current day's posts at the evening edition and caching them for the next morning, and a step that fills in images and reaction counts from a separate source. I cover these after the core three and the cost section.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The previous-day window: decided in a timezone, sent in UTC
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The default fetch window is the previous day in &lt;code&gt;TZ&lt;/code&gt;, from 00:00 to 24:00. &lt;code&gt;TZ&lt;/code&gt; can be set in the environment, and defaults to Asia/Tokyo if unset. By the time the morning edition goes out, "yesterday" is already over, so fetching the whole previous day gives a clearly bounded period.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can also give the window explicitly with &lt;code&gt;--from&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;--to&lt;/code&gt;. Write them in ISO 8601; values without a timezone are interpreted as &lt;code&gt;TZ&lt;/code&gt; local time. I use this for checks that span dates and for re-fetching.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;start_time&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;end_time&lt;/code&gt; sent to the API are in UTC. The window calculation looks like this (a simplified version to show the shape):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timedelta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timezone&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;zoneinfo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ZoneInfo&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;default_window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tz_name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="bp"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="bp"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;tz&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ZoneInfo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tz_name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;or&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tz&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;astimezone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tz&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;today_start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hour&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;minute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;second&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;microsecond&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;today_start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;timedelta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;today_start&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;astimezone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;timezone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;utc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;astimezone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;timezone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;utc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The period actually sent to the API is recorded in UTC in the output JSON's &lt;code&gt;window&lt;/code&gt;, so that later I can check which period a given JSON covers. The window calculation and parsing can be verified with a self-test that uses no network.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Configuration: keys and targets live in .env, and process environment values win
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Configuration goes in &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt;. There are three keys:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Key&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Required&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Meaning&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;X_BEARER_TOKEN&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Required&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Token used to authenticate with the API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;X_ACCOUNTS&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Required&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Target accounts, comma-separated, without &lt;code&gt;@&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;TZ&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Optional&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Timezone for the window; defaults to Asia/Tokyo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight conf"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;X_BEARER_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;=&lt;span class="n"&gt;xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;X_ACCOUNTS&lt;/span&gt;=&lt;span class="n"&gt;example_user_a&lt;/span&gt;,&lt;span class="n"&gt;example_user_b&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;TZ&lt;/span&gt;=&lt;span class="n"&gt;Asia&lt;/span&gt;/&lt;span class="n"&gt;Tokyo&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; is assumed to be a file you never publish. If a value with the same name exists in the process environment, that one takes precedence. That way the scheduler can override a value without rewriting the file.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Listing the target accounts in configuration makes it clear which accounts are being fetched. Cost estimates follow the same structure: multiply each account's post count by the post-read unit price, then add the user-read charges.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Fetching: resolve the user ID, paginate, and format the results into one JSON document
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fetcher follows these steps:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Resolve the user ID from the username (&lt;code&gt;GET /2/users/by/username/:username&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Call &lt;code&gt;GET /2/users/:id/tweets&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;start_time&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;end_time&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;max_results=100&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;tweet.fields=created_at,entities&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Follow &lt;code&gt;next_token&lt;/code&gt; in the response to paginate and get every post in the window.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a safety cap of 100 pages. If the cap is exceeded, the fetcher stops with an error telling you to narrow the window (in the code below the message reads "page limit reached; narrow the window", written in Japanese). It is a brake against pulling an unexpected volume.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The pagination skeleton looks like this (simplified):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch_posts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;start_utc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;end_utc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;max_pages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;start_time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;start_utc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;strftime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;end_time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;end_utc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;strftime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;max_results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;tweet.fields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;created_at,entities&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;posts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;max_pages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;api_get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/2/users/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/tweets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;posts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;extend&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]))&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;next_token&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{}).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;next_token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;next_token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;posts&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;pagination_token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;next_token&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RuntimeError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;ページ数の上限に達しました。窓を狭めてください&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The output is a single JSON document. It contains the fetch time &lt;code&gt;fetched_at&lt;/code&gt;, the period actually sent &lt;code&gt;window&lt;/code&gt; (UTC), and a list of posts per account. Each post carries &lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;created_at&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;urls&lt;/code&gt; (the links in the body, expanded), and &lt;code&gt;url&lt;/code&gt;. The values are fictional examples.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"fetched_at"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-11-06T21:00:05Z"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"window"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"start"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-11-05T15:00:00Z"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"end"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-11-06T15:00:00Z"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"accounts"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"example_user_a"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
      &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
        &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"1000000000000000001"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
        &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"created_at"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-11-06T03:12:45.000Z"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
        &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"text"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"（投稿本文がここに入る）"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
        &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"urls"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://example.com/page"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
        &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"url"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://x.com/example_user_a/status/1000000000000000001"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
      &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"example_user_b"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The newspaper reads only this JSON. It doesn't need to know anything about X's specifications or billing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When an error occurs, the fetcher prints a message that includes the HTTP status and exits non-zero. The token is never included in the output. The message adds a hint about the common cause:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;401&lt;/strong&gt;: invalid token&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;402&lt;/strong&gt;: out of credits&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;429&lt;/strong&gt;: rate limit&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since this component is called from a scheduler, failures show up through the exit code, and the message gives a lead on the cause.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cost management by window and count: about $1.25 a month, and a $10 failure
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cost control is mostly settled by the design so far (window and count). The X API uses pay-per-use billing. The unit prices I confirmed on 2026-11-07 on the X Developer Platform pricing page are below. Billing is counted by the number of resources fetched, not the number of requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Post reads: $0.005 each&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;User reads: $0.010 each&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prices can change, so check the pricing page when you use this yourself. Also, when the credit balance is empty, reads fail (that is the 402 above).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I estimate costs from the number of posts and users read within the chosen window, using these unit prices. For the newspaper's X fetching, the developer dashboard as of 2026-10-28 showed 247 billable events over the past 30 days, about $1.25 in total. "About $1.25 a month" is based on that point in time and that display. It is the result of fetching only the previous day's posts for two accounts every day, and it will change with the number of accounts and how much they post.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The other number is a real failure. On 2026-11-06, in work separate from the newspaper, I fetched the profile and post list of an account outside the target set, with no window and no cap on the count. About 1,000 items, including reposts, were read, and $10 was charged (the breakdown by read type was not kept in my records). Fetching without a window or a cap can incur charges on a very different scale from the daily fetch, all at once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The lesson is simple. &lt;strong&gt;With a pay-per-use API, decide the window and the count before you pull.&lt;/strong&gt; The fetcher's design reflects this: the window is required, and the page count has a cap. But the cap is only a brake on volume. Reading all 100 pages × 100 results would come to $50 per account in post-read charges at the listed unit price. What keeps the bill small is not the cap but narrowing the window and the targets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Refinement 1: fetch the current day early at the evening edition, then fetch only the remaining window the next morning
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now the two refinements I added while operating it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the morning edition fetches the previous day, it could pull the whole 00:00–24:00 span every time. But posts from 00:00 to 18:00 of that day were already fetched once to produce the evening edition (18:00). I save that fetched data as raw data in a per-date cache file and reuse it the next morning. The morning edition combines two parts:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The cached part (00:00–18:00)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fetching only the remaining window (18:00–24:00)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cache files are deleted once used. Even though the window is split in two, the parts add up to the previous day's 00:00–24:00, so this stays compatible with the design of deciding the window first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the posts dated 2026-10-30, for example, there were 4 cached posts and 4 fetched from the remaining window, for a total of 8.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Whether this reduces the morning fetch volume depends on how a day's posts are distributed across the day. I haven't measured the percentage reduction in my own setup, so I won't quantify the benefit here. The point is the division of work: fetch once at an intermediate point like the evening edition, and the morning only needs to pull a short window.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Refinement 2: fill in what the cards need with fxtwitter
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The other refinement is enrichment. This fetch method does not return the images, avatars, or reaction counts needed for the newspaper's cards. So I fill in the following from &lt;code&gt;https://api.fxtwitter.com/status/&amp;lt;post ID&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Images&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Avatars&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Counts of likes, reposts, and replies
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight http"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;GET https://api.fxtwitter.com/status/1000000000000000001
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The post ID is the &lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt; in the X API fetch result. I use the IDs the fetcher put into the JSON and enrich one post at a time. When I introduced this on 2026-10-26, the backfill succeeded for all 18 past posts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The roles are separate. The X API fetch provides the list of posts in the window, and the fxtwitter step adds the contents that go onto the card.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Who this design fits, and who it doesn't
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It fits a use like this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The target accounts are fixed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The period to fetch can be bounded by day or hour.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You want to hand the result to another system as JSON.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You don't want to add external libraries (it runs on the standard library alone).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It doesn't fit in these cases:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You want to search across topics or keywords. This component assumes fetching per account.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You need to predict billing precisely. Unit prices can change, and the number of posts in the window varies by day.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You need reliable fetching. I haven't collected statistics on fetch success rate or failure frequency. My operating records establish only that publication has continued and that the counts in the examples match; I don't yet have statistics that would support a numerical reliability guarantee.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Start with one account and one day
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first thing to do is start small:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Narrow the targets to one account.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fix the window at the previous day.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Multiply the number of fetched posts by the read unit price, add the user-read charges, and work out an expected cost.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Set the page cap by working backward from your own budget.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once the window and the count cap are fixed, the range your bill can swing over becomes predictable. After that, if you start using it regularly, adding an early fetch at an intermediate point, like the evening edition, plus enrichment with images and reaction counts, is enough.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my setup, these small, regular fetches cost about $1.25 a month, based on the dashboard snapshot dated 2026-10-28. Start with one day, set a window, and pull.&lt;/p&gt;




