&lt;li&gt;Results depend on prompt, seed, sampler settings and the base model version.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What this is about.&lt;/strong&gt; A tool I built for designing kayak colors grew into a t-shirt designer, the T-lab, on five of my sites. They all sell through one shop now.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why.&lt;/strong&gt; Run several sites on one codebase and sooner or later something has two truths. This is how I got back to one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A kayak with sleeves
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rebel Kayaks has a page where you paint, or design as it were, a kayak in proper RAL colours for factory coating. Then it occurred to me that a t-shirt is just a kayak with sleeves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A few sessions later the T-lab existed. You draw on a tee, pick a colour, pay at rugd.se, and a print shop prints it and posts it. I ordered one and it arrived, the right way up. As of this week it's also the only one ever ordered, which turned out to be excellent timing for tearing the plumbing apart. Because of course I didn't market it and of course I forgot to create a link for it on the site! Doing it now. Oh well :)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Five sites, five opinions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My opening message to the AI:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"the setup and infra sucks. It is way too complicated for any tenant admin, except me, with your help, to set up."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every site kept its own copy of the garment list, and every copy aged in its own way. Somebody forgot...maybe the rubber duck? One site was proudly offering a sports tee the shop no longer sold. You could design it lovingly and then not buy it. How does this happen, I hear you think? Ah, a quick idea, no particular plan for scaling. You know, make a POC and see if it flies. or in code lingo: &lt;em&gt;move fast and break things&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"The rugd shop can be an active "gate" but there can only be one truth."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The least trash wins
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI came back with three ways out. One T-lab for everyone, every site reading the shop live, or keeping the copies and syncing them harder. I asked for the one that "leaves the least trash in code".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the shop now says out loud what it sells, and every site listens. When the shop drops a garment, it vanishes everywhere at once. A site keeps only its own taste: which garments to offer, which colours to hide. A festival site can sell one tee. A shop site can sell all of them.&lt;br&gt;
Why this complicated, you think? Glad you asked! It is the exact same layout as a franchise. Now you suddenly know why it's worth it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The robot took the boring jobs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prices follow what printing really costs, plus a margin. Every night the shop checks its garments against the print shop and tidies up what changed. A cheeky 90% discount code now stops at what the shirt costs to make, instead of you paying people to wear your designs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It wasn't flawless. The nightly check did its job perfectly on the first night and told nobody, so for a morning I thought it had never run. Now it announces itself, like a cat that wants credit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then it shipped, one site at a time, because shipping everything at once has bitten me before. The old way of syncing went in the bin, and deleting things is my favourite kind of progress.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What happened, and how else it could be done
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Five sites each kept a copy of the truth. Now one shop holds it and the sites read it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Way&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Better at&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Costs&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One T-lab for every site&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Literally one copy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Every site loses its own look&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The shop pushes changes to each site&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Instant&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The shop has to know every site and chase the ones that are down&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Copies, synced now and then (what I had)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Works with the shop offline&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Two truths, and steps that get forgotten&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sites read the shop (chosen)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One truth, simple sites&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Each site leans on the shop; a cached copy covers short outages&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rule I'm keeping: if two places can disagree, they will. Make one of them a reader.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://idunworks.com/the-kayak-designer-learned-to-sell-t-shirts" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;idunworks.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  From a $40 HIV Shot to Walking Inside the Capsid: Why Structural Biology Needs Spatial ...
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From a $40 HIV Shot to Walking Inside the Capsid: Why Structural Biology Needs Spatial Computing  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This week the U.S. approved a generic version of lenacapavir—a long‑acting HIV prevention shot—with an Indian manufacturer pledging to supply low‑ and middle‑income countries at just $40 per year. That’s a remarkable milestone for global health equity. It’s also a testament to structural biology: lenacapavir is a first‑in‑class capsid inhibitor. Its mechanism? It binds the HIV‑1 capsid protein at the interface between hexamer subunits, stabilizing the lattice and blocking the nuclear entry and disassembly steps the virus needs to replicate.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For students, educators, and drug discovery scientists, that mechanism is usually encountered as a static ribbon diagram on a flat screen. A PDB entry (e.g., 7L5Z, 7L60). A publication figure. Maybe a PyMOL session if you have the time and compute.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What if you could step inside that capsid lattice at human scale?  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;NexoPeek&lt;/strong&gt; makes that possible. The Meta Quest application turns the headset into an interactive, 3D molecular laboratory. Rather than examining biological macromolecules on flat 2D monitors, users can step inside, walk around, grab, scale, and physically interact with 3D protein structures, ligands, and nucleic acids at human scale using bare hands or VR controllers. The app ships with twelve narrated tours across six thematic pillars—from Biotech Basics and Foundations of Life (hemoglobin oxygen binding, ATP production) through Pathways &amp;amp; Interactions (glycolysis, trypsin), Medicines in Action (aspirin COX inhibition), Genetic Technologies (CRISPR‑Cas9, Central Dogma, Yamanaka factors), and Green Biotech (plastic‑degrading enzymes). Each tour is grounded in real Protein Data Bank structures, turning abstract cellular mechanisms into tangible, walk‑through experiences.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Under the hood the runtime is built on Unity 2022 LTS with the OpenXR 1.1 subsystem, targeting Quest 2, Quest Pro, and Quest 3. The rendering pipeline uses a custom URP‑based shader that streams Mol*‑generated geometry directly to the GPU, keeping the frame budget under 11 ms (≈90 FPS) on Quest 2 and under 8 ms on Quest Pro. Hand‑tracking latency sits at ~12 ms thanks to the Quest Interaction SDK’s low‑level hand‑pose buffer; controller input adds another 3 ms. All heavy lifting—structure parsing, symmetry expansion, electron‑density volume upload—runs on a background Job‑System thread pool, so the main thread stays free for UI and passthrough compositing.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For advanced research, the Professional PDB Viewer suite provides a connected scientific workstation. Smart Search &amp;amp; Database Routing queries RCSB PDB, UniProtKB, AlphaFold DB, SWISS‑MODEL, and PDBe via smart keyword cascades and UniProt gene synonym resolution, with automated model validation. Local data ingestion supports PDB, CIF, MOL2, GRO, XYZ, DX, TC, and XTC files, letting users overlay electrostatic field‑line maps, custom bond definitions, and electron‑density volumes. Publication‑quality graphics are achieved through six one‑tap &lt;strong&gt;Mol* Presets&lt;/strong&gt; derived from MolViewSpec concepts, supporting cartoon, ball‑and‑stick, spacefill CPK, molecular surface, B‑factor heat, and tube‑trace representations. A typical preset configuration looks like:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"preset"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"surface-bfactor"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"colorScheme"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"bfactor"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"surfaceOpacity"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"clipPlane"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"enabled"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"normal"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"distance"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;12&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Granular sequence and chain control lets you scroll per‑chain amino‑acid sequences, tap individual residue “pills” to project a 3D visual halo, pin custom atoms, or isolate specific chains. On‑board analytics include AI Pocket Prediction (cloud‑based PrankWeb/P2Rank or an offline TensorFlow‑Lite model that runs entirely on‑device), Structural Alignment via an integrated Foldseek pipeline to evaluate domain function conservation and drug‑swapping hypotheses, Ligand Inspector with PubChem integration to scan hetero‑compounds, evaluate hydrogen‑bond networks, and fetch 3D ligands, and a Distance Calculator that trigger‑picks two atoms in 3D space to report exact angstrom measurements. A Room‑View toggle floats 3D molecular structures into the user’s real physical room while keeping the wall menu operable—mixed‑reality passthrough that keeps the scientific workstation grounded in the user’s environment.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I still remember the first time I launched the capsid tour in my cramped campus office. The morning light slipped through the blinds as I slipped the Quest Pro over my eyes, the faint hum of the HVAC the only soundtrack. The HIV‑1 capsid (PDB 7L5Z) materialised at waist height, a geodesic lattice of hexamers and pentamers glowing in a soft cyan. I reached out—no controller, just my bare hand—and the hexamer under my fingertips pulsed, each subunit highlighted in amber. A narration whispered, “Lenacapavir locks this interface,” and a tiny ligand appeared, snapping into the cleft like a key.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My graduate student, Maya, peered over my shoulder, her coffee steaming. “Can we see the electrostatic map?” she asked. Two taps later the field‑line overlay bloomed, red‑blue ribbons tracing the charge landscape that guides the inhibitor. We walked around the capsid, crouching to peer into the central channel, then stepping back to watch the whole assembly rotate. The Room‑View toggle kept the lab bench visible—my notebook, a half‑eaten bagel, the whiteboard scrawled with kinetic equations—so the molecule never felt like a detached hologram.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Later that week I used the same session in a 30‑minute undergraduate lecture. The students, seated in a semicircle, each donned a Quest 2. I guided them through the same tour, but now the narration paused at each subunit, letting them “grab” a hexamer and feel the resistance of the stabilized lattice. One student, Jorge, whispered, “I finally get why the drug stops the virus from uncoating.” His eyes widened when the distance calculator showed a 3.2 Å hydrogen bond between the inhibitor carbonyl and a conserved asparagine—numbers he’d only ever seen in a table.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The experience turned abstract concepts into tactile moments—something a flat screen never could. The mixed‑reality Room View kept the classroom grounded; the professional viewer let me pull up the same structure later, run a Foldseek alignment against a bacterial capsid, and export a Mol* preset for a manuscript figure—all without leaving the headset.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you teach, research, or simply wonder how a $40 shot can block a virus at the molecular level, NexoPeek offers a hands‑on gateway that respects the science and the scientist. It is not a medical device; it is a visualisation platform that empowers better‑informed decisions.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.meta.com/experiences/nexopeek/1453797551140347/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.meta.com/experiences/nexopeek/1453797551140347/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been developing software for over 25 years. I've worked with old COBOL systems, modern web applications, financial platforms, and pretty much every generation of technology in between.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And there's something that has always bothered me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why the hell are we still rewriting the same financial rules?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every time someone starts a new fintech, ERP, banking application, or financial SaaS, the story repeats itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How should we round money? What happens when two operations modify the same financial record? Can an approval become invalid? What should happen when a transaction fails halfway through? How do we preserve audit evidence?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Different companies. Different architectures. Different programming languages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Same fundamental questions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And somehow, we keep answering them from scratch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  So I started digging.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I went back to old books, financial systems, legacy implementations, regulations, and years of experience working with financial software.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Looking for the rules that should never have been reinvented in the first place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I found wasn't a shortage of solutions. It was a shortage of shared, precise, verifiable foundations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We have countless libraries, frameworks, platforms, and architectures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But how often do we agree on what the correct financial behavior should actually be?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's where FinKore started.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Meet FinKore.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://finkore.dev" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;FinKore&lt;/a&gt; is an open-source initiative to define a common foundation for financial software.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's not another framework or SaaS.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's not an Lib you install and magically solve your financial problems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;FinKore is about defining the rules before writing the code.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea is simple: fundamental financial behavior should be documented, explicit, testable, and independent of programming languages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your application might be written in TypeScript, Java, Go, or something that hasn't even been invented yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The underlying financial rules shouldn't change just because your technology does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  So Rules first. Code after.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;FinKore is organized around three fundamental layers:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CoreDoc -&amp;gt; Specifications -&amp;gt; Conformance&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CoreDoc&lt;/strong&gt; establishes the financial principles and obligations. This is where we explain &lt;em&gt;why&lt;/em&gt; a rule exists.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Specifications&lt;/strong&gt; translate those principles into precise technical requirements, defining expected behavior, constraints, and failure conditions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conformance&lt;/strong&gt; provides portable test cases with inputs and expected results, allowing implementations to be evaluated against the same requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the idea behind FinKore.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not to tell you which framework to use, but to establish what behavior your implementation should guarantee.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Five domains. One shared foundation.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;FinKore currently focuses on five foundational domains:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Money:&lt;/strong&gt; Exact calculations, currency, rounding, allocation, and serialization.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Workflow:&lt;/strong&gt; Valid state transitions, lifecycle behavior, and rejection rules.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Audit:&lt;/strong&gt; Preserving evidence of what happened, who acted, and what must remain traceable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Approvals:&lt;/strong&gt; Decisions, conditions, approval validity, and the facts behind authorization.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Financial Entities:&lt;/strong&gt; Identity, validation, revisions, and controlled changes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are not complete banking products or accounting systems. They are building blocks that financial applications can use to establish predictable behavior.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And importantly, FinKore doesn't decide your business policies for you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It defines the foundation. Your application defines its own business rules, architecture, and integrations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What about AI?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If an AI doesn't know which rounding policy applies, when an approval expires, or how an operation must behave after rejection, it will make assumptions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And assumptions are dangerous in financial software.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;FinKore provides explicit specifications and machine-readable operation contracts that can help developers and AI-assisted workflows work from defined expectations instead of inventing behavior.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AI should implement financial rules, not invent them.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  I'm not trying to build this alone.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is probably the most important part.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I don't believe one developer, one company, or even one engineering team has all the answers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A developer understands implementation constraints. An accountant understands financial obligations. An auditor understands evidence and controls. A financial architect understands how these requirements interact across systems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;FinKore needs all of these perspectives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's why the project is open-source and community-driven.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I want people to challenge the specifications, find missing scenarios, question assumptions, propose edge cases, and help make the foundation stronger.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't need to contribute code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes a difficult question from an auditor is more valuable than a thousand lines of implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  We're just getting started.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;FinKore already has published specifications, portable conformance cases, documentation, and a growing set of discussions about financial behavior.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But I see this as the beginning, not the finish line.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I want FinKore to become a shared reference that developers can rely on when building financial products, regardless of the technology they choose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not because everyone should write the same code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But because we shouldn't have to reinvent the same financial foundations forever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Your product may be unique. Your financial rules don't have to be.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've worked with financial software, I'd love to hear what rules you've had to implement over and over again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And if you disagree with something in FinKore, even better. Open a discussion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's how we improve it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Explore the project:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://finkore.dev" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://finkore.dev&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GitHub:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/willgittens/FinKore" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/willgittens/FinKore&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Define once. Build many times.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://aicoding-guide.com/en/posts/weekly-2026-11-08/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://aicoding-guide.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The change with the widest reach this week is that &lt;strong&gt;auto mode no longer falls back to the built-in classifier&lt;/strong&gt;. Where telemetry or feature flags are off, including behind an LLM gateway, calls the server cannot check are &lt;strong&gt;asked about or denied&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For unattended runs, &lt;code&gt;permissions.denyInsteadOfPrompting&lt;/code&gt; is new: instead of stalling on a prompt, the call is denied and the turn continues.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key point&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
This week in brief&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Auto mode asks about or denies calls the server can't check, with no classifier fallback&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;permissions.denyInsteadOfPrompting&lt;/code&gt; replaces a stalled prompt with a denial&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Web and shell tool calls now ask before sending your account email address&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Claude Code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three releases: 2.1.317, 2.1.318 and 2.1.319.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Auto mode lost its fallback (2.1.319)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The CHANGELOG reads: "Changed auto mode with telemetry or feature flags off, including behind LLM gateways: calls the server can't check are asked about or denied, with no fallback to the built-in classifier; set &lt;code&gt;CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_MODE_SERVER=0&lt;/code&gt; to keep using it (Bedrock, Vertex and Foundry are unchanged)."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Environment&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Effect&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Telemetry or feature flags off&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Calls the server can't check are asked about or denied&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Behind an LLM gateway&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The same&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Bedrock / Vertex / Foundry&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Unchanged&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_MODE_SERVER=0&lt;/code&gt; restores the previous behaviour of using the built-in classifier. In the other direction, &lt;code&gt;CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_MODE_SERVER=1&lt;/code&gt; turns the server-side check on behind a gateway even with &lt;code&gt;CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_EXPERIMENTAL_BETAS&lt;/code&gt; set — and where managed settings set the latter, they must set the &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt; too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Relatedly, &lt;code&gt;/status&lt;/code&gt; no longer reports "Auto mode server: Enabled" when the server-side review is not actually reaching the session. For how the modes differ, see &lt;a href="https://aicoding-guide.com/en/posts/claude-code-default-mode/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Claude Code starts in auto mode by default&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  New settings and variables (2.1.319)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Setting&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it does&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;permissions.denyInsteadOfPrompting&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Denies a call that would wait on a permission prompt and continues the turn, in terminal sessions, subagents and teammates&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_FEATURE_FLAGS&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stops feature-flag fetching without turning telemetry off; every flag uses its built-in default&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_MCP_TASK_BACKGROUND&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Waits for an MCP task inside its tool call instead of backgrounding it immediately&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;MCP tasks are now supported in the terminal: when a server runs a tool call as a task, Claude Code moves it to the background right away and reports the result when it finishes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Disabling feature flags also stops idle compaction&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;code&gt;CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_FEATURE_FLAGS&lt;/code&gt; stops flag fetching while leaving telemetry on. One of idle compaction's conditions is that Claude Code can fetch feature flags from Anthropic, so setting this variable stops it running. For the full list of conditions, see &lt;a href="https://aicoding-guide.com/en/posts/claude-code-autocompact-window/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Making Claude Code compact earlier, or never&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  A check before your email address goes out (2.1.317)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;New in 2.1.317: "Added a check that asks before a web or shell tool call sends your account email address to the network."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three security fixes also landed:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A &lt;code&gt;sed&lt;/code&gt; command could skip the permission check in accept-edits and auto mode (2.1.319)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bash deny and ask rules missed commands written with quoted words (2.1.319)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;/rewind&lt;/code&gt; could restore or delete a file through a directory symlink (2.1.319)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Sign-in expiry is now visible (2.1.318)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This one matters for unattended runs. The expiry date of a stored claude.ai sign-in is now available as &lt;code&gt;loginExpiresAt&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;claude auth status&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;credential_expires_at&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;claude -p&lt;/code&gt; JSON results, with a one-line stderr warning starting 10 days before.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The release itself is mostly fixes for configuration files and stored logins being corrupted on network home directories, so it is worth taking if you run on a shared home. Claude Code also no longer installs or updates the IDE extension when started from an IDE terminal; use &lt;code&gt;/ide&lt;/code&gt; from an outside terminal instead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Codex
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;v0.167.0 ships six features.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Addition&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it does&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;codex agents --no-daemon&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Opens the agent command center; it and the session picker now mark unread results&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Backend switching&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Switch between connected backends from the terminal session picker, keeping drafts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Image preview&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Preview local image attachments while composing, on supported terminals&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;gcp_setup_wizard&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sets up Google Cloud Vertex AI with Application Default Credentials or a credential file&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;System proxy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Opt into macOS or Windows system proxy settings from &lt;code&gt;/experimental&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Patch details&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Expand patch details in the transcript while an approval prompt is open&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;gcp_setup_wizard&lt;/code&gt; continues from v0.165.0, which made Vertex AI a built-in provider via &lt;code&gt;model_provider = "gcp"&lt;/code&gt; — see &lt;a href="https://aicoding-guide.com/en/posts/codex-update-0-165/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Codex CLI v0.165 makes Vertex AI a built-in provider&lt;/a&gt;. The API key and cloud provider options moved under the "Sign in another way" menu.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three fixes stand out. Saved chats stay visible after switching model providers, and a chat that cannot resume can still be read and turned into an editable prompt for a new chat. Network approval prompts close when their requests end, and concurrent requests to the same endpoint prompt one at a time. Automatic context compaction now accounts for retained output from failed responses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Gemini CLI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;v0.67.0 (November 4) has two features among 27 entries: the &lt;code&gt;models&lt;/code&gt; command, and the fix for a Claude hook timeout that migrated 1000x too small. See &lt;a href="https://aicoding-guide.com/en/posts/gemini-cli-update-0-67/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Gemini CLI v0.67 adds a models command and fixes the unit in Claude hook migration&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What to check now
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If you run auto mode behind an LLM gateway, note the 2.1.319 change and set &lt;code&gt;CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_MODE_SERVER&lt;/code&gt; if needed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For unattended runs that must not stall, consider &lt;code&gt;permissions.denyInsteadOfPrompting&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check your sign-in expiry with &lt;code&gt;claude auth status&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Move to 2.1.318 if you run on a network home directory&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Summary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Auto mode asks about or denies calls the server can't check, with no classifier fallback&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_MODE_SERVER=0&lt;/code&gt; restores the old behaviour; Bedrock, Vertex and Foundry are unchanged&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;permissions.denyInsteadOfPrompting&lt;/code&gt; turns a stalled prompt into a denial&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Web and shell tool calls ask before sending your account email address&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Codex 0.167.0 adds &lt;code&gt;gcp_setup_wizard&lt;/code&gt; and opt-in system proxy settings&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
